{{Capacity and the archaeological record}}

!! How sharing works

A plot worked by n households produces base-yield × n^alpha, so each household's share is base-yield × n^(alpha − 1). A plot can admit n households only if `base-yield >= need × n^(1 − alpha)`. Occupancy of a plot becomes a count instead of a 0/1 flag, and a household releases a plot only if it actually holds one. Settlement placement still excludes farmed land, as in the published model.

!! Controls added

|!Control |!Default |!Meaning |
|`crowding-limit` |1 |Maximum households per plot (slider 1 to 4). 1 is the published one-household-per-plot rule. |
|`intensification-return` |0.5 |alpha, the exponent on labor (slider 0 to 1). alpha = 1 means no diminishing returns; alpha < 1 means each added household yields less. |

All [[Baseline]] controls are also present.

!! Reporters added

* `plot-capacity` (patch reporter): households a plot can feed, from the admission condition above.
* `potential-crowded` and `capc-series`: crowding-adjusted carrying capacity, now and per year. Equal to `potential` when `crowding-limit` = 1.
* `mean-crowding`, `max-crowding`: mean and maximum households per occupied plot. 1.0 means no crowding occurred.
* `peak1-crowded`, `peak2-crowded`, `crowded-peak-ratio`: the peak-window measures of Janssen (2009, section 4.13) applied to `capc-series`.

!! BehaviorSpace experiments

None in this version. The crowding experiments are in [[Combined]], where they can be run with `soil-on?` OFF.

